\section*{Primitive-root infinitude for every admissible base}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 029; 2026-10-04), the asserted result is that each integer other than $-1$ or a square is primitive modulo at least $c_a x/(\log x)^2$ primes between $x$ and $2x$ for large $x$.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 029; 2026-10-04), cubic theta coefficients under Kummer twists give a Hecke zero-free comparison. Controlled prime predecessors in fixed progressions are then sieved against complete-splitting obstructions, retaining enough primes on which the base has full multiplicative order.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The integer-base result supplies infinitely many primitive-root primes with a quantitative dyadic-interval lower bound. This addresses the infinitude target, but complete coverage is not established by this catalogue and the claim remains partial.

\subsection{Formal verification}
No Lean formalization is listed at this snapshot for manuscript 1 (family 029; 2026-10-04).

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

